    "processes": ["compound", "sound-change", "ateji"],
    "headline": "けさ is “this morning” — but the 今 in it is a stand-in for an older “this”.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese. Originally a compound of 此 (ke, “this”) + 朝 (asa, “morning”)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "The initial /ke/ is an alternative form of the initial /ko/ (此, “this”) seen in words such as ここ (koko, “this place → here”) or これ (kore, “this [nominal] → this”), and the final /sa/ is a contraction from */ke.asa/ to /kesa/ with the medial -a- falling out, as Old Japanese did not permit vowel clusters."
      },
      {
        "quote": "It is the same element found in 今日 (kepu→kyō, “today”)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "The use of the kanji 今 (“now”) instead of 此 (“this”) is an example of jukujikun (熟(じゅく)字(じ)訓(くん))."
      }
    ],
